import Verso
import VersoManual

open Verso.Genre
open Verso.Genre.Manual

#doc (Manual) "Open questions" =>

This section lists what remains open about $`F_0`, with the status of each
question and the evidence there is.  PROVED, REFUTED and OPEN are used in the
sense of the rest of the document; evidence that is not a proof is reported as
evidence.

# The questions answered

* _Is $`F_0` dense?_  No: PROVED, seven covers at the bottom (Section 3).
* _Does the open interval of every pair contain a thread?_  No: PROVED, the
  open interval of a cover is empty (Section 3).
* _Does $`\mathbb{Q}` order-embed in $`F_0`?_  Yes: PROVED, explicitly and
  without `Classical.choice` (Section 5).
* _Does $`\bigcirc` extend to $`\mathrm{DM}(F_0)` as a nucleus?_  Yes: PROVED,
  and the extension is the greatest one (Section 8).
* _Is $`{\downarrow}t_n` finite for every $`n`?_  No: REFUTED for $`n = 6, 7`
  and every $`n \ge 9`; finite for $`n \le 5` (Sections 3 and 9).
* _Is $`F_0` order-embedded in a proper interval of itself?_  Yes: PROVED,
  $`\sigma` embeds it in $`[t_3, \top]`, and the copies
  $`\sigma^{n}(F_0)` are nested and strictly decreasing (Section 10).

# Open

* _Is $`\sigma` onto $`[t_3, \top]`, that is, is $`F_0 \cong [t_3, \top]`?_
  OPEN.  The image $`\sigma(F_0)` is the subalgebra generated by $`t_3`: the
  closure of $`\{t_3\}` under $`\land`, $`\lor`, $`\twoheadrightarrow` and
  $`\bigcirc`.  The question is whether every element of $`[t_3, \top]` lies in
  it.
* _Are the bases $`\sigma^{n}(\bot)` cofinal in $`F_0`, or do they stop below a
  cut that $`F_0` does not fill?_  OPEN.  They are strictly increasing
  (Section 10).
* _Which bases $`a` give a faithful copy $`\varphi \mapsto \varphi[\bot := a]`?_
  OPEN.  $`\bigcirc a \ne a` is necessary (Section 10), and $`a = t_3` works.
* _Is $`{\downarrow}t_8` finite?_  OPEN.  The chain below $`t_6` does not reach
  it, as $`t_6 \nvdash t_8` (Section 9).
* _Is the nuclear extension of $`\bigcirc` to $`\mathrm{DM}(F_0)` unique?_
  OPEN.  Uniqueness fails for some Heyting algebras: REFUTED in general
  (Section 8).
* _Which pairs have a thread?_  For every pair of lattice polynomials in the
  odd gaps the open interval contains $`\aleph_0` threads, pairwise
  $`\parallel`: PROVED (Section 7).  A cover has none.  Whether the open
  interval of an arbitrary pair $`(u, v)` that is not a cover contains a
  thread: OPEN.
* _Ropes._  As defined in Section 2, connectedness by zigzags gives a single
  rope in every interval with more than four elements (a pen argument, not
  mechanised).  Two refinements are under consideration, (a) width, by
  pairwise $`\parallel` families of threads, and (b) the pointwise order
  between threads; the choice between them is open.
* _Is the interval of some pair dense, that is, free of covers?_  OPEN.  A
  candidate is $`[g_i \land g_j, g_i]` with $`i < j`.
* _$`\mathrm{DM}(F_0)` as a model._  That it is a complete algebraic model of
  PLL of the cardinality of the continuum, with $`\aleph_0` mutually
  incomparable copies of $`\mathbb{R}`, is a pen argument (Section 8); as
  mechanised statements these are OPEN.
* _Choice._  The rung truth sets, the single-edge lifts and the holes of
  Sections 6 and 7 carry `Classical.choice` in their proofs; whether those
  results can be proved without it has not been examined, except for the odd
  truth sets (re-proved without it in Section 4, and used so in Section 9).
